{
 "content": [
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "The multinomial is an example of a ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "italic"
      }
     ],
     "text": "parametric",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " distribution for multiple random variables. The multinomial is a gentle introduction to joint distributions. It is a extension of the binomial. In both cases, you have ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "n",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " independent experiments. In a binomial each outcome is a \"success\" or \"not success\". In a multinomial there can be more than two outcomes (multi). A great analogy for the multinomial is: we are going to roll an ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "m",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " sided dice ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "n",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " times. We care about reporting the number of outcomes of each side of your dice.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Here is the formal definition of the multinomial. Say you perform ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "n",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " independent trials of an experiment where each trial results in one of ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "m",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " outcomes, with respective probabilities: ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "p1,p2,…,pm",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " (constrained so that ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "∑ipi=1",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "). Define ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "Xi",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " to be the number of trials with outcome ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "i",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ". A multinomial distribution is a closed form function that answers the question: What is the probability that there are ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "ci",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " trials with outcome ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": "i",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ". Mathematically:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n          \\P(&X_1=c_1,X_2 = c_2, \\dots , X_m = c_m) \\\\\n          &= { {n} \\choose {c_1,c_2,\\dots , c_m} }\\cdot p_1^{c_1} \\cdot p_2^{c_2}\\dots p_m^{c_m} \\\\\n          &=  { {n} \\choose {c_1,c_2,\\dots , c_m} }\\cdot \\prod_i p_i^{c_i} \n          \\end{aligned}"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "justify"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "This is our first joint random variable model! We can express it in a card, much like we would for random variables:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Multinomial with Three Outcomes",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "sourceBytes": 6276,
      "sourceSha256": "92af14496835d3de350abd7215be7dedc7a58d31d398e139d54dc13268a626cd"
     },
     "type": "interactive-demo"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Of course, a Multinomial could describe more than three outcomes:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Multinomial Joint Distribution (General)",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "sourceBytes": 2398,
      "sourceSha256": "29bfd2b304d16c4a15e281cdaffdba6ad9198f99f7a4c5318a8f9c1989ff9855"
     },
     "type": "interactive-demo"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "examples",
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Examples",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Standard Dice Example:",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
         },
         "type": "textStyle"
        }
       ],
       "text": "A 6-sided die is rolled 7 times. What is the probability that you roll: 1 one, 1 two, 0 threes, 2 fours, 0 fives, 3 sixes (disregarding order).",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": " ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\P(X_1=1,X_2 = 1&, X_3 = 0,X_4 = 2,X_5 = 0,X_6 = 3) \\\\&= \\frac{7!}{2!3!}\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^1\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^1\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^0\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^2\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^0\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^3\\\\\n&=420\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^7\n\\end{aligned}"
     },
     "type": "block-tex"
    }
   ],
   "type": "purpleBox"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        },
        {
         "type": "italic"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Weather Example:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Each day the weather in Bayeslandia can be {Sunny, Cloudy, Rainy} where ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "p_{\\text{sunny}}=0.7"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": ", ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "p_{\\text{cloudy}}=0.2"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " and ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "p_{\\text{rainy}}=0.1"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": ". Assume each day is independent of one another. What is the probability that over the next 7 days we have 5 sunny days, 1 cloudy day and 1 rainy days?",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n  \\P(X_{\\text{sunny}}=5,X_{\\text{rainy}} = 1&, X_{\\text{cloudy}} = 1) \\\\\n  &= \\frac{7!}{5!1!1!} (0.7)^5 \\cdot (0.2)^1 \\cdot (0.1) ^1 \\\\\n  &\\approx 0.14\n\\end{aligned}"
     },
     "type": "block-tex"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "justify"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": " How does that compare to the probability that every day is sunny?",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n  \\P(X_{\\text{sunny}}=7,X_{\\text{rainy}} = 0&, X_{\\text{cloudy}} = 0) \\\\\n  &= \\frac{7!}{7!} (0.7)^7 \\cdot (0.2)^0 \\cdot (0.1) ^0 \\\\\n  &\\approx 0.08\n\\end{aligned}"
     },
     "type": "block-tex"
    }
   ],
   "type": "purpleBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "The Multinomial is especially popular because of its use as a model of language. For a full example see the ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "./federalist",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Federalist Paper Authorship",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " example.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "derivation-using-many-dice-rolls",
    "level": "2",
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Derivation Using Many Dice Rolls",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "When we first learned about the Binomial, we derived it using the ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "./many_flips",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "many coin flips example",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": ". To derive the Multinomial Distribution, we can extend that metaphor to consider the probability of different outcomes of rolling many ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "type": "bold"
      }
     ],
     "text": "dice",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": ".",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "In this example we are going to imagine that we have a dice that can only have three outcomes. When you roll it you could get an A, a B or a C. What an odd dice! In order to make this metaphore as general as possible I am going to let our dice be even stranger! Imagine the probability of each of the three outcomes is not equally likely. Instead let ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "p_A"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " be the probability of rolling an A, and so on. To make things concrete image:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\np_A = 0.6\\\\\np_B = 0.1\\\\\np_C = 0.3\n\\end{aligned}"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "First here is a simulator where you can try rolling this dice 10 times:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "type": "bold"
        }
       ],
       "text": "Dice Roll Simulator",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "sourceBytes": 2450,
      "sourceSha256": "a6cc7fa92f804416c82801344a11b8144dfcf73e65cd7d740f0c314c53aae79f"
     },
     "type": "interactive-demo"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "exactly-4as-1b-and-5cs",
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Exactly 4As, 1 B, and 5 Cs",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Imagine you roll 10 dice, where each dice has three sides {A, B, C}. Here are all the orderings of outcomes for the 10 rolls where you get exactly 4 As, 1 B and 5 Cs:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "sourceBytes": 41218,
    "sourceSha256": "cf12ab49e476b68a59bc71ef63fba10efe54aa1d87bc6083f8e9f1862219af37"
   },
   "type": "interactive-demo"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Let's call each of these rows an \"ordering\" since each row is a unique way to order the 4 As, 1 B and 5 Cs. Let ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "N"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": " be the number of unique orderings.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Exactly how many unique orderings are there of 10 letters with 4 As, 1 B and 5 Cs? We can solve this problem using ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "/draft/combinatorics/#perm_indisctinct",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "Permutations of Indistinct Objects",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "color": "rgb(33, 37, 41)"
       },
       "type": "textStyle"
      }
     ],
     "text": ":",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Num Orderings} = \\frac{10!}{4! \\cdot 1! \\cdot 5!} = {10 \\choose 4, 1, 5} = 1260\n\\end{aligned}"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Each of these orderings can be thought of as one event, or outcome, of rolling 10 dice. Let's name ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "E_i"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " to be the event that we get the exact outcome in the ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "i"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": "th row.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Let's calculate the probability of ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\p(E_{17})"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ". Eg:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "language": "python"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "(A, A, A, C, A, C, C, C, C, B)",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "codeBlock"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "In order to calculate the probability of this row, we need to know the probability of A, B, and C on our dice. Recall that:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\np_A = 0.6\\\\\np_B = 0.1\\\\\np_C = 0.3\n\\end{aligned}"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "The the probability of getting exactly the result in row 17 is going to be:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\p(E_{17}) &= p_A \\cdot  p_A \\cdot  p_A \\cdot  p_A \\cdot  p_B \\cdot  p_C \\cdot p_C \\cdot p_C \\cdot p_C \\cdot p_C \\\\\n&= p_A ^ 4 \\cdot p_B \\cdot p_C^5\n\\end{aligned}"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "In fact that is the probability of every single row! Now we are ready to calculate the probability of exactly 4 As, 1 B and 5 Cs:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\p(&\\text{exactly 4 As, 1 B and 5 Cs}) \\\\\n\t&= \\sum_{i=1}^N \\p(E_i) && \\text{Mutual Exclusion}\\\\\n\t&= \\sum_{i=1}^N p_A ^ 4 \\cdot p_B \\cdot p_C^5 && \\text{Sub in }\\p(E_i) \\\\\n\t&= N \\cdot p_A ^ 4 \\cdot p_B \\cdot p_C^5 && \\text{Sum $N$ times}  \\\\\n\t&= {n \\choose n_A, n_B, n_C} \\cdot p_A ^ 4 \\cdot p_B \\cdot p_C^5 && \\text{Perm of indistinct objects}\\\\\n    &= {10 \\choose 4, 1, 5} \\cdot 0.6 ^ 4 \\cdot 0.1 \\cdot 0.3^5 && \\text{Substitution}\\\\  \n\\end{aligned}"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  }
 ],
 "type": "doc"
}
